+### Preventive maintenance schedules
+
+A schedule (`asset`, `title`, `priority`, `interval_days`, `next_due`, `active`) belongs to an asset;
+its site is the asset's current site. `POST /api/schedules/{id}/generate` raises an ordinary `open`
+work order (`description` `"Preventive maintenance"`, the schedule's title and priority, requested by
+the caller, `schedule` = the schedule's id), advances `next_due` by `interval_days` and emits
+`preventive_generated`. Generated orders then follow the work order workflow above.
+
+| Rule | Location |
+|---|---|
+| who may create / PATCH / DELETE / generate (managers; supervisors of the asset's site) | `permissions.can_create_schedules`, `can_manage_schedules_for`; `services._ensure_manages_schedules_for` |
+| fields, `next_due` date check, `interval_days` 1-365 | `validation.clean_schedule`, `SCHEDULE_CREATABLE/EDITABLE`, `iso_date` |
+| no p4 on high-criticality assets, unknown/retired asset on create | `services.create_schedule`, `update_schedule` (`_ensure_priority_allowed`) |
+| generate: 403, then 409 (inactive, retired asset, unfinished generated order, before `next_due` - 7 days), then 400 | `services._authorize_generate` (`GENERATE_LEAD_DAYS`), `generate_from_schedule` |
+| the `generate` form on the detail page | `services.schedule_actions` (runs `_authorize_generate`) |
+| delete refused once it generated an order; asset delete refused while it has schedules | `services.delete_schedule`, `delete_asset` |
+| retiring an asset deactivates its schedules (un-retiring does not reactivate) | `services.update_asset` (`repository.deactivate_asset_schedules`) |
 
